## 8. Literature Review

### 8.1 Surveys and framing

* **Tiwari et al. IEEE Access 2021 — ML in Financial Market Surveillance:** defines two abuse families (price manipulation: spoofing, wash, ramping; insider trading via classified info), notes scarcity of labels, imbalance, sequential nature, need for contextual/collective anomaly. Compares supervised (k-NN, trees, ANN, SVM) when labels exist vs unsupervised (LOF, COF, k-NN global) + active anomaly detection for triage. Finds regression-tree ensembles robust for price prediction → abnormal price detection. Directly justifies your ensemble + human-in-loop.
* **Maheshwari & Chatnani 2023 — SupTech SLR 55 papers 1999-2022:** SupTech detects manipulation/insider/fraud and forecasts crises; empowers supervisors. Calls for shared datasets.
* **Khodabandehlou & Golpayegani 2022; Yi et al. 2023:** manipulation/fraud detection systematics; RNN-ensemble, RF excel on volumes/returns/volatility features.
* **Annunziata 2026 — Market Abuse and AI (RegTech/SupTech):** EU MAR analysis. Four surveillance families: (i) rule-based (your RB), (ii) pattern recognition ML, (iii) network analysis, (iv) text mining NLP. Supervised + unsupervised complementary. Reviews AMF ICY, BaFin ALMA, SEC MIDAS, CSA MAP, ASIC MAI, NASDAQ SMARTS/NICE/Scila. Concludes MAR needs interpretation + supervisory practice update, not wholesale rewrite, but AI raises accountability/transparency/cybersecurity issues. Your POC is textbook (i)+(iv) hybrid.
* **Priyadarshi & Kumar JCSS 2024 — Insider detection with AI review:** ML/DL promising but data heterogeneity + cross-country regulatory variance persist; need standardized datasets + collaboration.

### 8.2 Econometrics: event studies, volume/volatility, Consob MAD

* **Brown & Warner 1985; Boehmer et al. 1991; Corrado 1989; Kolari-Pynnonen 2011:** market-model CAR, standardized cross-sectional test, rank tests for thin trading/asymmetry/variance increases. FTSE-350 study (PMC 2022) shows methodology matters: 240d estimation, FTSE-350 index proxy, both BMP parametric + Corrado non-parametric; finds much smaller insider abnormal returns (sales -0.75% post) once large-cap controlled — warns against overstating profitability.
* **Volume event studies:** Cready-Ramanan log-volume power, Campbell-Wasley mean/market-model abnormal log-turnover, Karafiath GLS vs OLS (OLS sufficient, heteroskedasticity-robust stats essential), Yadav review, EventStudyTools/ R EventStudy package (`VolumeModel`, `VolatilityModel`). Corporate events often show volume/volatility spikes without net price move — exactly why Step 1 price-only misses cases.
* **Baker-Gelbach 2020 (ML for single-firm event studies in litigation):** regularization helps variance/power modestly; bigger wins from peer index + SQ robust test. Prescription for your market adjustment.
* **Minenna/Consob QDF54 — Detection of Market Abuse quantitative approach:** tripwires from theory + supervisory experience + observed cases: autocorrelated volumes per participant, diffusion-process abnormal returns (alternative to market model), joint interpretation into warnings with temporal validity. Validated on Consob insider/manipulation cases. Your Steps 1-3 are a simplified Consob tripwire set; read QDF54 for calibration method.
* **Mitchell-Netter (SEC disgorgement):** market-model abnormal return × position = illicit profit proxy for sanctions. Useful for materiality threshold (€1M notional) justification.
* **Stylized facts:** Meulbroek 1992 (higher volumes + abnormal returns on insider days, herd effect), Cornell-Sirri 1992 (insiders hide in small trades), Bhattacharya et al. 2000 Mexico (pre-announcement leakage fully anticipates, no disclosure effect), Chakravarty 1999 Carnation (disclosure effect exists but insider vs uninformed price impact inseparable), Bagliano et al. Italy (no autocorrelation change). Implication: look for small but persistent directional build + volume from followers, not just block trades.

### 8.3 Supervised ML for insider/unlawful trading

* **Deng et al. 2019/2021 (DCZ) — Intelligent system China:** PCA + RF on 26 indicators, recall 83.87% 60d window.
* **2025 RF extension to US (Comp. Economics):** 9.6M Form-4 + CRSP + Compustat, 110 features (20 numeric à la DCZ + governance: IsDirector/Officer/10%Owner, Acquisition/Disposition), 3984 balanced transactions, RF 95%+ accuracy, permutation + hierarchical Spearman clustering for importance. Key predictors: governance, performance, market risk. Caveats: balanced lab sample overstates production precision; feature engineering + data quality dominate. Lesson: once you have labels, governance + risk features help; start logging them now.
* **Golmohammadi et al. DSAA 2014; Wang et al. Neurocomputing 2019:** NN/SVM/trees/RNN-ensemble for manipulation (pump-dump, spoofing) on returns/volumes/volatility, daily + tick. Relevant if you expand beyond TAME to manipulation typologies later.

### 8.4 Unsupervised anomaly + networks (core for internal data)

* **Mazzarisi et al. EPJ Data Science 2024 — Consob/Italian takeovers 2019-2021 investor-resolved:** two complementary unsupervised methods around PSEs: (1) k-means clustering discontinuity vs own past + vs peers; (2) statistically-validated co-occurrence networks for synchronized groups (insider rings: directional trading before PSE). Human-in-loop: seed from (1) → check clique in (2). Built to mimic Consob investigator reasoning + court-tested cases. **Closest methodological precedent to your Step 2 upgrade + ring extension.**
* **Mazzarisi et al. 2024 arXiv:2403.00707 — PCA/Autoencoders contextual anomaly:** reconstruction paradigm on full position time-series per investor per PSE; flag if max error day in investigation window + infrequent activity or crowdedness condition + net buying at PSE (position_T - position_0 >0.5). Data-driven thresholds (bimodal score → local minimum, top decile for crowdedness). Overcomes arbitrary 3-feature summaries. Directly implementable on IMAD panel.
* **General anomaly:** Isolation Forest (FCA TechSprint winner for false-positive reduction), LOF/COF/LoOP for local vs global, LSTM/TCN autoencoders for sequences, Bayesian nets for sparse anomalies (FCA DHI), econophysics kinetic-energy order-book models (KCL/UCL), entropy-based layering detector (SteelEye). FCA TechSprint 2024 solutions catalogue is a playbook: parameter-free auto-calibration, eyeDES contextual alerts + feedback retraining loop, eflow flexible parameterization, Conatix time-series classification with explainability, Kaizen multimodal.
* **Change-point:** PELT, BOCPD, CUSUM for mean/variance shifts in delta/volume; GARCH(1,1) for volatility regime (EventStudy Dieselgate vignette uses GARCH for AR/volume/volatility).

### 8.5 NLP / LLM for finance and surveillance (core for event confirmation)

* **Financial language models:** FinBERT (Araci/Prosus), FLANG, PIXIU, InvestLM, **BloombergGPT 50B** (FinPile + Pile, best on ConvFinQA/FiQA/FPB/Headline, NER second). Finding: fine-tuned encoder (BERT/RoBERTa/DeBERTa) beats zero-shot GPT-3.5/4 on narrow finance classification; GPT-4 wins on broad/diverse news with good prompting (EMNLP-FinLMEval, MDPI 2025 GPT vs FinBERT +10% with prompt engineering, 2024 compact-vs-GPT study with market-reaction labels). **Implication:** use FinBERT-class for cheap high-recall pre-filter, GPT/Claude-class with few-shot for nuanced price-sensitivity + explanation.
* **FANAL 2024 (BERT + ORPO):** 12 financial categories (M&A, Bankruptcy, Dividend, Debt Default, Public/Private finance, etc.), silver labels via XGBoost + 1200 manual, ORBERT calibration for imbalance. Beats GPT-4o/Llama-3.1/Phi-3 few-shot on most classes, cheaper. FinDeBERTa HF extends to 18 labels with per-label P/R (Dividend 1.0, M&A 0.89). Use taxonomy for `eventType` field.
* **Event causality:** FinCausal 2022, DEMAMD; LLM-augmented LLaMA + graph reasoning +5-10 F1 on multi-cause, handles implicit/multi-hop causality (rate hike → market dip → FX). Relevant for protracted processes and ripple attribution.
* **FinRipple ACL 2025:** time-varying KG + adapter + RL with asset-pricing theory to predict cross-entity ripple; relaxes single-company event-study assumption. Needed for macro vs idiosyncratic disambiguation and for Stellantis-type supply-chain spillovers.
* **LSEG Surveillance Guide (AWS Bedrock + Claude Sonnet 3.5, Sep 2025):** production-grade reference. 250k RNS 6 months, EDA, 110 expert-annotated 9-point scale, two-step prompt, summary + classification + CoT justification, 6-week pilot: 100% precision NOT, 100% recall SENSITIVE (never misclassify sensitive as not). Benefits: triage time, consistency, audit trail, scale. Roadmap: add financials/market data, few-shot/fine-tune, live side-by-side validation. **Copy this evaluation + deployment pattern.**
* **FCA TechSprint SteelEye transcript:** vector DB for trader/instrument relationships (correlation, sector, LLM description similarity) + entropy layering score + LLM verification with explainability out-of-box; two LLMs disagree → use agreement as confidence; fine-tune ambition. Pattern: deterministic anomaly → LLM verifier/explainer.
* **Nordgren 2025 thesis — LLM alert scoring:** synthetic price-ramping/circular-trading datasets, 7-32B open models vs Gemini 2.5 Flash, zero/few-shot, self-correction/consistency, doc access, graded 1-10 vs binary, AUC + Mann-Whitney. Result: few-shot significantly helps, others not; self-consistency raises AUC via variance reduction; graded > binary for ranking; ~14B best open size. **Adopt: few-shot + 1-10 + self-consistency.**
* **ACM 2025 ethical judgment benchmark (73 FMSB enforcement →1971 Taguchi variants, 10 LLMs):** larger models more cautious but still incentive-sensitive (profit framing, obfuscation raise approval of suspicious trades). Warning: don't let LLM approve trades; constrain to evidence summarization with oversight.

### 8.6 Explainability, trust, human-in-loop

* **Frontiers 2022 (FIRM Round Table) + FIN-TECH:** SHAP clustering (Bussmann et al.), plausibility checks for critical decisions, stakeholder-specific explanations (analyst vs supervisor vs client). Model-agnostic post-hoc + intrinsic transparency.
* Active anomaly detection + learning-to-rank (AP-based) for imbalanced alert lists with equal-weight false-positive problem.

### 8.7 SupTech in production (what peers run)

* Core engines still NASDAQ SMARTS, NICE Actimize, Scila, Trading Technologies — indicator-based, need adaptive ML overlay for volumes/data growth.
* Supervisors: MIDAS (SEC, order-book analytics), MAI (ASIC, real-time algorithmic, reduced search time), MAP (CSA), ICY (AMF), ALMA (BaFin). Trend: anomaly detection + surveillance models + text mining combined.

