## 10. Risks, Model Risk, EU AI Act, Governance

### 10.1 Model-risk framing (SR 11-7 / EBA / ECB)

Under SR 11-7 (Fed/OCC 2011, confirmed 2024 to cover AI/ML/LLM apps) and EU equivalents (EBA GL 2021/05 internal governance, ECB Guide to Internal Models 2024, EBA ML-for-IRB 2021/2023, BCBS 239), any quantitative surveillance model is a **model**: needs inventory tiering, conceptual soundness doc, independent validation (separate from developers), outcomes analysis on held-out/production, implementation controls, ongoing monitoring with revalidation triggers, board reporting. Vendor LLM is not exempt — bank remains responsible, must do own outcomes analysis + challenger.

For surveillance (non-capital) tiering is typically medium, but STOR failure = regulatory breach → elevate to high attention.

### 10.2 EU AI Act (Reg. 2024/1689, in force Aug 2024)

* **Classification:** creditworthiness scoring of natural persons = high-risk Annex III 5(b) (obligations from 02/12/2027 per Omnibus 2026/1744). **Fraud detection expressly carved out** from 5(b). TAME surveillance is fraud/abuse detection, not credit scoring, and scores issuers/desks, not natural persons' creditworthiness → **not high-risk under 5(b)** on current reading. No automatic high-risk tag.
* **Caution:** Commission high-risk guidelines due 02/02/2026; profiling of natural persons (trader-level suspicion scoring) could raise fundamental-rights scrutiny. Treat as **limited-risk + internal high-stakes**: voluntarily apply high-risk disciplines proportionally — risk management Art.9, data governance/bias Art.10, logging Art.12, transparency Art.13, human oversight Art.14, accuracy/robustness/cybersecurity Art.15, QMS Art.17 — via existing MRM file (EBA mapping 2025 finds no contradiction, recommends integrated file, not parallel).
* **Deployer duties:** follow provider instructions, ensure competent oversight with real override (monitor override rate for automation bias), keep logs, FRIA-style impact note if trader-level profiling. Art.86 explanation right applies to credit decisions, not directly here, but analyst-facing explanations still required for audit.

### 10.3 DORA, GDPR, MAR confidentiality

* DORA ICT risk, logging, incident reporting for pipeline + LLM endpoint.
* GDPR: minimize personal data in prompts; trade-desk pseudonymization; STOR personal data handled under MAR legal basis, not in LLM training.
* No training on internal data by external provider; contract + technical (no-log, VPC) guarantees.

### 10.4 LLM-specific risks and mitigations

* Hallucinated events/citations → RAG with URL + timestamp + quote; NLI entailment check (summary must be entailed by retrieved text); refuse if no source.
* Source capture bias (English only, missing Euronext filings) → bilingual queries, include regulatory feeds.
* Prompt injection via news (malicious press release instructing LLM) → system/user separation, no tool auto-execution from retrieved text, output JSON schema validation.
* Data poisoning/adversarial (Art.15) → allowlist publishers, checksum feeds, monitor sensitivity distribution drift.
* Over-reliance → graded score + mandatory analyst checkbox + override log; track precision/recall by analyst.

### 10.5 Validation and audit artefacts (to produce)

Model doc (concept, data, methodology, limitations, use), validation report (independent), back-test report, prompt/model registry, retrieval logs, Excel versioning, monitoring dashboard (alert counts, precision, latency, LLM refusal/disagreement rate), change log, incident runbook.

